   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="address">
                    <ab>
                        <address>
                            <addrLine rend="printed">
                                <name type="geo" key="G_FRA_PARI_16">2, SQUARE ROBIAC</name>
                            </addrLine>
                            <addrLine rend="printed">192, RUE DE GRENELLE</addrLine>
                            <addrLine>Tel. Segur 95-20</addrLine>
                        </address>
                    </ab>
                </div>
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <p>Dear <name type="person" key="budg82">Budgen</name>: Will you dine with us and <name type="person" key="weav76">Miss Weaver</name> tomorrow (Saturday)? I want you to <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">meet</seg> her. If so, call for me about 7.30 and we'll walk first. Ring me up if you can conveniently.</p>
                    <closer>
                        <salute>sincerely yours</salute>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">James Joyce</name>
                        </signed>
                        <dateline>
                            <date when="1928-01-27">Friday</date>
                        </dateline>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">It appears that this meeting never took place, since in <name type="work">Dear Miss Weaver: Harriet Shaw Weaver 1876-1961</name> (New York: Viking, 1970), Jane Lidderdale and Mary Nicholson report that when in August 1933 Frank Budgen delivered to her a sketch of the Liffey at Chapelizod, they “had not yet met” (328).</note>
            </div>
      </body>
   </text>
